The one-line form of the XML writer has no line endings to write

XmlElement::writeTo() turns the line endings off by passing a null
newLineChars, and says so by starting the indentation level at -1. That is the
form XmlElement::TextFormat::singleLine() asks for, and the form
AudioProcessor::copyXmlToBinary() writes the state of every plug-in in.

writeElementAsText() then breaks the pair: a child that comes after a text node
is written at indentation level 0, whatever level its parent had, and at level 0
the guards let the null newLineChars reach
operator<< (OutputStream&, const char*), which hands it to strlen():

  modules/juce_core/streams/juce_OutputStream.cpp:379:33: runtime error: null
  pointer passed as argument 1, which is declared to never be null

The smallest document that shows it is <a>t<b><c/></b></a>: text, and then an
element that has an element of its own inside it. UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
stops there; a build without it reads address zero, or, since the compiler may
take the parameter's nonnull for granted, writes nothing at all.

The child now keeps the level of a parent that has no line endings, so the
guards mean what they say again. Where there are line endings, nothing changes.

A fuzz target of ADLplug-Next found it, one that gives the plug-in's
setStateInformation() the text of a project's state: it wraps the text the way
a host keeps it, with copyXmlToBinary(), and a state that holds text between
its elements is enough.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by
cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is
upstream.

diff --git a/modules/juce_core/xml/juce_XmlElement.cpp b/modules/juce_core/xml/juce_XmlElement.cpp
index 9cebaf0f15..aa664dff28 100644
--- a/modules/juce_core/xml/juce_XmlElement.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_core/xml/juce_XmlElement.cpp
@@ -314,9 +314,13 @@ void XmlElement::writeElementAsText (OutputStream& outputStream,
                     if (indentationLevel >= 0 && ! lastWasTextNode)
                         outputStream << newLineChars;
 
-                    child->writeElementAsText (outputStream,
-                                               lastWasTextNode ? 0 : (indentationLevel + (indentationLevel >= 0 ? 2 : 0)), lineWrapLength,
-                                               newLineChars);
+                    // An indentation level below zero says that there are no line endings
+                    // to write, and newLineChars is then null, so a child must not be
+                    // given a level of its own that would ask for one.
+                    const auto childIndentation = indentationLevel < 0 ? -1
+                                                : (lastWasTextNode ? 0 : indentationLevel + 2);
+
+                    child->writeElementAsText (outputStream, childIndentation, lineWrapLength, newLineChars);
                     lastWasTextNode = false;
                 }
             }
